# origami_crane.pwmodel - a folded paper crane (orizuru), built from explicit
# vertex and triangle buffers.
#
# What this example is for:
#   * `append_buffers` with real data. No boolean of primitives makes a crane: the
#     form is flat panels meeting at hard creases, and the creases are the design.
#   * the readability argument for this format. A crane as a triangle soup is 200
#     unlabelled numbers. Here it is seven named panels, each one `append_buffers`
#     line under a comment that says which fold it is, so moving the beak means
#     editing three numbers on the line that says BEAK.
#
# THE FOUR FEATURES THAT MAKE IT A CRANE RATHER THAN A DART
# Every one of these was got wrong at least once, and each time the model came back
# reading as a glider or an insect. They are the acceptance criteria, not decoration:
#
#   1. THE NECK RISES. It leaves the body's front shoulder going UP and FORWARD at
#      42 degrees. A neck that comes forward and DOWN is an aircraft nose-gear leg,
#      and that single sign error is what read as "a glider".
#   2. THE TAIL RISES TOO, and mirrors it - up and BACK at 40 degrees, about the same
#      length. The tail is the neck's counterweight; without it the bird has a front
#      and no back. A short vertical spike is a mast, not a tail.
#   3. THE WINGS ARE SHORT AND CREASED. Root chord 88 against a 124-long body,
#      rising 17.8 degrees, each one a quad whose w0-w2 DIAGONAL is a real fold
#      because the quad is deliberately non-planar. Long flat swept wings are an
#      aerofoil, and that is the third thing that read as "a glider".
#   4. THE BODY IS A DEEP KEEL that comes to a point at the bottom. That point plus
#      the two rising spikes is the whole orizuru silhouette.
#
# HOW THE PAPER IS BUILT
# A sheet of paper is a plane, and a plane has no thickness, so a panel authored as
# a flat polygon would be invisible edge-on and would z-fight against itself along
# every crease. Every panel here is authored FLAT - the way it is drawn on the
# unfolded square - and `extrude` then gives the whole part its paper thickness in
# one op. That is why the vertex tables below are 2D outlines you can read, rather
# than 3D shells you cannot.
#
# WHY `extrude` HAS NO `face_direction`
# Counter-intuitive, and worth the paragraph. `face_direction` selects faces by
# normal, and the docs warn that omitting it selects the whole mesh, "which
# DUPLICATES a closed solid rather than thickening it". These parts are OPEN
# sheets, not closed solids - and for an open sheet duplicate-and-wall IS
# thickening, and it is the only path that produces a correct closed shell here.
# Passing a matching `face_direction` instead gave a DEGENERATE result: the wings
# came out at 14 triangles instead of 24, one wing missing its skin entirely.
# So the whole-mesh path is deliberate, not an oversight.
#
# WHY `direction` SETS THE FACING, AND THE RULE THAT FOLLOWS
# It does. An older paragraph here - and `model.authoring` with it - claimed the sign
# of `direction` "only chooses which side of the authored surface the material lands
# on". That claim shipped this file with an inside-out `sheet`. The mechanism, read
# off the engine rather than inferred from a render:
#
#   * `OffsetMeshRegion.cpp:696-705` displaces THE ORIGINAL TRIANGLES by
#     `direction * distance`. The originals keep their authored winding and end up on
#     the +`direction` face of the new slab.
#   * `OffsetMeshRegion.cpp:734-744` reverses the STATIONARY DUPLICATE, which is the
#     -`direction` face.
#
# So the slab is outward-facing exactly when the authored facing normal points ALONG
# +`direction`, and inside-out when it points against it. THE RULE:
#
#     extrude direction == the part's own facing normal (NOT the -RHR flip of it)
#
# `wings` obeyed it from the start - facing -Z, `direction=(0, 0, -1)`. `sheet` did
# not: facing -Y against `direction=(0, 1, 0)`.
#
# HOW TO MEASURE IT, BECAUSE A NORMAL RENDER CANNOT SEE IT
# A closed shell renders IDENTICALLY inside-out. Backface culling shows you whichever
# wall faces you, the two walls carry opposite normals, and they sit 4 uu apart - so
# every A/B of the `direction` sign comes back a no-op (measured: mean luminance
# 0.035767 vs 0.035771), and every opposed-camera silhouette comes back complete.
# Those two tests cannot fail, which is why they were quoted twice as proof and why
# the defect survived two reports.
#
# The test that DOES work is to delete the `extrude` and render the bare panels from
# both sides. A one-sided panel is invisible from behind, so the side it shows on IS
# its facing normal, measured rather than derived. Done here: with the extrude gone,
# `sheet` disappears completely from +Y and draws in full from -Y (facing -Y), while
# `wings` do the opposite. Restore the extrude afterwards.
#
# The second test shows the RULE rather than the facing, and is worth building once:
# take one panel, extrude it thick enough to hold a camera (distance=2000, then
# `translate_mesh` to recentre) and shoot from inside. With `direction` along the
# facing normal the mesh vanishes - every wall back-facing, you see straight out to
# the backdrop. Against it you are standing in a lit corridor. Same geometry, same
# bounds, one sign apart.
#
# THE BLACK PANEL: THREE CAUSES, AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME BUG
# "Inverted normals on the crane" has been reported three times and has had three
# different answers. Separate them before fixing anything.
#
#   1. THE `sheet` SHELL REALLY WAS INSIDE-OUT, from the first version until the
#      `direction=(0, -1, 0)` fix below. Every vertical part - keel, neck, head,
#      beak, tail - is in `sheet`, which is exactly the set the third report named.
#      A shaded render CANNOT see this (above), and neither can `health`: an
#      inside-out shell is still closed, still manifold, still 0 boundary edges.
#      What it DOES break is anything that reads winding rather than shading -
#      the mesh distance field most of all, whose inside/outside sign is decided
#      purely by counting backface hits (`MeshDistanceFieldUtilities.cpp:261-281`).
#      An inverted shell inverts the field, and Lumen/DFAO then light the part as
#      though the camera were inside it. In a lit scene that reads as - precisely -
#      inverted normals, on the sheet parts only, with the wings looking fine.
#   2. A CAST SHADOW. The near wing shadows the body. Turning `CastShadows` off on
#      the key light alone lit the bottom half of the body straight back up - so more
#      than half the dark area was never a facing question at all. Test a suspicious
#      dark region this way first; it costs one property write.
#   3. THE WING UNDERSIDE, a genuine DOWNWARD-FACING surface under lights that are
#      all above. It survives the shadow test as a hard-edged dark quadrilateral and
#      is bounded by the dihedral - see the wing table for why 17.8 and not 26.
#      Unlike (1) this one IS visible to a shaded render, and only to one: not to
#      `health`, not to a triangle count, not to an orthographic silhouette.
#
# Only (1) is a normals bug, and `flip_normals` / `recalculate_normals` are the wrong
# tool even for it: the honest fix is the `direction` sign, at the op that got the
# facing wrong. (2) and (3) are not repaired by touching normals at all.
#
# NEITHER OF THE OLD "PROOFS" WAS VALID. The opposed-camera silhouette test proves a
# part is not a single-sided sheet; it says nothing about the winding of a closed one.
# The `direction` A/B measured 0.00007 of mean luminance and was read as "the sign
# does nothing", when what it actually shows is that this defect is invisible to that
# measurement. Use the delete-the-extrude test above instead.
#
# THE SIGN THAT CATCHES EVERYONE
# Unreal's front face is the NEGATION of the right-hand rule: for a triangle
# (v0, v1, v2) the familiar (v1-v0) x (v2-v0) gives a vector, and the face you can
# actually see points the OTHER way. Every table below is wound so that the
# right-hand-rule normal is +Y for `sheet` and +Z for `wings` - so the FACING normals
# are -Y and -Z, and those are the two numbers the `extrude` lines have to match.
# `VectorUtil::Normal` in the engine spells the negation out: it returns
# `(V2-V0) x (V1-V0)`, commented "Unreal has Left-Hand Coordinate System".
#
# Panels being consistent WITH EACH OTHER is necessary but is not the whole test, and
# reading it as the whole test is what shipped the inside-out sheet: ONE inconsistent
# panel renders as a black hole among white ones and is easy to spot, while a
# UNIFORMLY inverted part renders exactly like a correct one and is invisible. Check
# consistency, then check each part's facing against its `extrude direction`.
#
# For a `sheet` panel the test is arithmetic you can do in your head. With every
# vertex at y = 0 the cross product collapses to a single term, so for (v0, v1, v2)
# with e1 = v1 - v0 and e2 = v2 - v0 the right-hand-rule normal is +Y exactly when
#
#     e1.z * e2.x  -  e1.x * e2.z  >  0
#
# which is the same thing as listing the outline CLOCKWISE in (x right, z up). Every
# sheet table below reads clockwise: down the front, along the belly, up the back,
# across the top. Check the sign rather than trusting the pattern.
#
# THE TWO PLANES
# A folded crane is flat except for its wings: body, neck, head and tail all lie in
# the bird's centre plane. So there are two parts, one per plane:
#   `sheet` - everything at y = 0, thickened along Y.
#   `wings` - the two spread wings, thickened along Z.
#
# The wing ROOT walls are buried on purpose. Each wing's inboard end is a rectangle
# spanning x -44..44 at z 70..72, and the body outline contains that whole rectangle,
# so the wall never reaches a surface a camera can see. Parts are MERGED, not
# unioned, so anything a part leaves poking out of another part stays poking out -
# burying the root is what makes two parts safe here.
#
# Triangle indices are LOCAL to their own `append_buffers` call - index 0 is that
# call's first vertex, not the mesh's. Panels are therefore independent: adding a
# fold does not renumber the ones above it.
#
# ONE SLOT, AND `append_buffers` CAN NAME IT
# An older paragraph here said explicit-buffer geometry could not be given a slot NAME,
# only a `material_id=` INDEX. That is FALSE. `append_buffers` carries both, and they
# are mutually exclusive (verified against `model.describe_ops`, not quoted):
#   * `material="Paper"` - a slot NAME, which is what `pwmodel 0` binds by. Slot
#     identity is the name, model-wide, exactly as on every generator op.
#   * `material_id=2` - a RAW index that BYPASSES the slot table, so it can never
#     satisfy a `materials { }` binding no matter what the block says.
# So a named binding is available here and always was reachable; do not repeat the
# old claim, and do not hand-manage indices to work around it.
#
# THE BLOCK USED TO BE ABSENT, ON THE ARGUMENT THAT "A ONE-SLOT MODEL GAINS NOTHING
# FROM NAMING ITS ONE SLOT". That was true only while the alternative was a stock
# engine material. Untagged geometry lands in a synthetic `Default` slot bound to
# `WorldGridMaterial`, so the price of not naming the slot was a paper crane that
# rendered as grey grid - and there is no way to reach the binding to change it,
# because the slot the compiler invented is not one this file mentions. Naming the
# slot is what makes the crane paper-coloured.
#
# ONE SLOT IS STILL CORRECT. A crane folded from one square is one material, and the
# temptation to split the wings or the beak off should be resisted: a slot boundary is
# a SHADING BEHAVIOUR boundary, and there is only one surface on this object. What
# varies - if anything ever does - is `color=`, which is a tint within the slot.
#
# THE COLOUR IS ON `set_vertex_color`, NOT ON THE PANELS, AND THAT IS FORCED.
# `append_buffers` takes `material=` but NOT `color=`: it already carries a per-vertex
# `colors=` buffer, and a scalar `color=` would write every vertex and silently
# overwrite it. So seven `colors=` buffers or one `set_vertex_color set_all=true` -
# and for a single flat paper tone the op is the honest one. It is safe here because
# neither part contains a boolean; `set_all` does not reach geometry a boolean has
# contributed as a tool (see the note in amphora.pwmodel).
#
# If you ever DO add a second material - a printed wing, a gilt beak - add its name to
# the block AND put `material="<Name>"` on the `append_buffers` lines that want it: a
# block whose names no op references compiles with PWMODEL_UNUSED_MATERIAL ("the
# binding is dropped") and does nothing.

pwmodel 0

materials {
    # One sheet of paper, one shading behaviour, one slot. Matte: paper has a dry,
    # open surface with almost no specular roll, and a satin binding here reads as
    # foil or as plastic - which is the one thing a folded-paper example must not do.
    Paper = "/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/MI_PwModelExample_MatteDielectric"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The centre plane: body, neck, head, beak, tail. Authored flat at y = 0 in
# (x, z), x forward toward the beak, z up.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
part sheet {
    # --- BODY -------------------------------------------------------------
    # A six-sided keel, listed clockwise from the neck root: front shoulder, down the
    # breast, the belly POINT at the origin, up the back, the tail shoulder, and over
    # the ridge peak. 124 long and 100 deep - almost square, which is what stops it
    # reading as a fuselage.
    #
    # v2 IS A POINT, not a flat keel, and that is the change that made the silhouette
    # a bird. The earlier body carried a 30-wide flat bottom so the model would stand
    # up unaided; it also gave the whole thing the flat belly of an aircraft. A crane
    # is displayed on a stand or in the air, so the point wins and the flat is gone.
    #
    # v5, the ridge peak at z=100, stands 28 above the wing roots at z=72. That gap is
    # the crane's back showing between the wings and it is load-bearing - see THE BLACK
    # PANEL in the header.
    append_buffers vertices=[(62, 0, 66), (36, 0, 16), (0, 0, 0), (-36, 0, 16), (-62, 0, 66), (0, 0, 100)] triangles=[(0, 1, 2), (0, 2, 3), (0, 3, 4), (0, 4, 5)] material="Paper"

    # --- NECK -------------------------------------------------------------
    # ONE straight tapering strip, not two panels with a bend in them. Up and forward
    # at 42 degrees, 22 wide at the root and 14 at the tip, rooted at (56, 62) so the
    # inboard half is inside the body. A crane's neck is a single unbroken fold; the
    # two-panel version with a kink in the middle read as a bird's neck in profile and
    # as a bent wire from anywhere else.
    #
    # 42 degrees is the number to change. Below about 25 it stops reading as a crane
    # and starts reading as a goose in flight; above about 55 the head sits behind the
    # wings and the bird looks like it is rearing.
    append_buffers vertices=[(49, 0, 70), (146, 0, 153), (156, 0, 143), (63, 0, 54)] triangles=[(0, 1, 2), (0, 2, 3)] material="Paper"

    # --- HEAD -------------------------------------------------------------
    # The reverse fold that turns the neck tip into a head, and the ONLY place the
    # line changes direction: the neck runs up at +42 and the head runs forward and
    # down at -20. That break is what a reverse fold looks like, and it is why the
    # neck can be a single panel.
    #
    # 24 wide against a 14-wide neck tip. Broader than the neck so that neck and beak
    # do not read as one continuous spike - but only just. An earlier head was 42 wide
    # against a 20-wide neck and read as a paddle nailed to the end of a stick.
    #
    # THE HEAD MUST OVERLAP THE NECK, AND FOR TWO RELEASES IT DID NOT. The head panel
    # used to start at (152, 134) / (160, 156), which is 5.8 PAST the neck tip edge
    # (146, 153)-(156, 143): the two quads missed each other by about 3.6, so the head
    # and beak were a DETACHED island floating off the end of the neck, and a camera
    # that put the joint against the sky saw daylight through the notch. Nothing in
    # `health` says so - the mesh is still closed with 0 boundary edges, because each
    # island is closed on its own; the only tell is `componentCount`, and a count of 7
    # on a model with 2 parts and 2 wings was read for two releases as "the parts are
    # separate", which it is not.
    #
    # Both tables below are therefore shifted 9 BACK along the head's own -20 line,
    # (-8.5, +3.1). The head's line, width and length are untouched; only where it
    # starts moved. That buries the neck's tip corner (156, 143) inside the head quad
    # and the head's back corner inside the neck strip, so the two interpenetrate over
    # an area instead of chasing an exact meeting - the same rule the tracery in
    # `gothic_window` is built on. Keep at least ~4 of overlap if these numbers move.
    append_buffers vertices=[(143.5, 0, 137.1), (151.5, 0, 159.1), (171.5, 0, 149.1), (165.5, 0, 132.1)] triangles=[(0, 1, 2), (0, 2, 3)] material="Paper"

    # --- BEAK -------------------------------------------------------------
    # One triangle off the head's front edge, on the head's own -20 line so beak and
    # head are one straight run. 44 long. Move v1 alone to re-aim it; keep v0 and v2
    # apart or the jaw goes needle-thin.
    #
    # v0 and v2 ARE the head's front edge and must stay equal to it vertex for vertex,
    # which is what keeps beak and head one welded island. They moved back with the
    # head by the same (-8.5, +3.1) - see the note there - so the beak tip is now at
    # x = 209.5 and that, not 218, is the model's +X bound.
    append_buffers vertices=[(171.5, 0, 149.1), (209.5, 0, 125.1), (165.5, 0, 132.1)] triangles=[(0, 1, 2)] material="Paper"

    # --- TAIL -------------------------------------------------------------
    # The neck's counterweight, and deliberately built to the same recipe: a straight
    # tapering strip out of the opposite shoulder, up and BACK at 40 against the neck's
    # 42, 120 long against the neck's 128, 26 wide at the root against the neck's 22.
    # Near-symmetry is the point - the two spikes read as one fold seen twice, which is
    # exactly what they are on the unfolded square.
    #
    # It is 26 wide rather than the neck's 22 because the tail is never foreshortened
    # by a head on the end of it, and at 18 it read as a length of wire taped to the
    # back.
    append_buffers vertices=[(-64, 0, 52), (-153, 0, 133), (-143, 0, 145), (-48, 0, 72)] triangles=[(0, 1, 2), (0, 2, 3)] material="Paper"

    # Paper thickness. No face_direction - see the note at the top of the file.
    #
    # `direction` MUST POINT ALONG THE PANELS' OWN FACING NORMAL, which for these
    # tables is -Y (see THE SIGN THAT CATCHES EVERYONE). It read (0, 1, 0) for two
    # releases and that is what shipped the whole sheet - keel, neck, head, beak,
    # tail - as an INSIDE-OUT shell. See WHY `direction` SETS THE FACING.
    extrude distance=4 direction=(0, -1, 0)
    # Re-centre: the extrude grew the sheet into -Y only.
    translate_mesh translation=(0, 2, 0)

    # xatlas, not box: the panels face every which way once folded, and a box
    # projection smears the beak and the tail point across the same island.
    # Kozo paper: warm off-white, never pure white. A 1.0 sheet clips flat under any
    # key light and the whole point of this model - a dihedral crease you can read as
    # a change of shade - disappears with it.
    set_vertex_color set_all=true color=(0.93, 0.90, 0.83, 1)

    uv channel=0 mode=xatlas

    # Origami is defined by its creases. 0 degrees splits every edge, so no facet
    # borrows its neighbour's normal and each fold stays a hard line.
    split_normals split_angle=0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The wings. Authored in (x, y) with the dihedral already in z - the wing rises as
# it goes out - so a single extrude along Z thickens both.
#
# The left wing is the right wing's table with y negated AND the vertex order
# reversed. Negating one axis flips the winding and reversing puts it back, so the
# two tables have the same right-hand-rule normal (+Z) and the pair is a true
# mirror. Keep both edits together: change one table and the crane goes lopsided,
# which is the one thing origami never is.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
part wings {
    # --- RIGHT WING -------------------------------------------------------
    # A QUAD, AND THE DIAGONAL IS A REAL FOLD. w0-w3 is the root crease, buried in the
    # body at y = 0 (see THE TWO PLANES). w1 is the shoulder of the leading edge and
    # w2 the tip. The four points are deliberately NOT coplanar, so the w0-w2 diagonal
    # that the triangle pair shares comes out as a genuine dihedral - a crease line you
    # can see under any light, which is the "visible crease" a folded wing needs and a
    # planar quad cannot give you at any triangle count.
    #
    #     root chord   88   against a 124-long body: SHORT. This is a folded flap.
    #     span        106   per side
    #     rise      72->106 across the span, which is 17.8 degrees
    #     tip z       106   against the body ridge at 100 - level with it, not over it
    #
    # THE DIHEDRAL IS BOUNDED AT BOTH ENDS AND BOTH BOUNDS WERE HIT. At 12 degrees the
    # pair read as one flat shelf capping the body and the model came back as a glider.
    # At 26 the tips stood 24 clear of the ridge, and because a camera at the model's
    # own mid-height then looks UP at the near wing, its underside filled the middle of
    # every eye-level frame as a hard-edged dark panel - which is what has twice been
    # reported as inverted normals. 17.8 puts the tips level with the ridge, which is
    # also where a real orizuru carries them.
    #
    # w1 IS OFF THE w0-w2-w3 PLANE ON PURPOSE, BY ABOUT 19. That is the entire crease.
    # Those three points define a plane on which y = 3.118 * (z - 72); at w1's z of 98
    # the plane wants y = 81, and w1 sits at y = 62. Drop w1 onto the plane - it is an
    # easy "tidy-up" to make - and the two triangles become coplanar, the shared w0-w2
    # diagonal stops being a dihedral, and the wing loses its fold and reads as sheet
    # metal. If you retune the wing, re-check that offset rather than the shape.
    append_buffers vertices=[(44, 0, 72), (26, 62, 98), (-18, 106, 106), (-44, 0, 72)] triangles=[(0, 1, 2), (0, 2, 3)] material="Paper"

    # --- LEFT WING --------------------------------------------------------
    append_buffers vertices=[(-44, 0, 72), (-18, -106, 106), (26, -62, 98), (44, 0, 72)] triangles=[(0, 1, 2), (0, 2, 3)] material="Paper"

    # Downward, so the authored z values stay the wing's upper surface.
    #
    # THE SIGN OF `direction` DOES SET THE FACING, and -Z is the correct one here: the
    # wing tables' right-hand-rule normal is +Z, so their FACING normal is -Z, and the
    # rule is `direction` == facing (see WHY `direction` SETS THE FACING in the header).
    # Flip it to (0, 0, 1) and the wings go inside-out, exactly as `sheet` was.
    #
    # An earlier note here said the sign "does not set the facing", on an A/B that gave
    # mean luminance 0.47222 vs 0.47229. That A/B was real and the reading of it was
    # wrong: a closed shell renders identically inside-out, so the measurement can only
    # ever come back a no-op. It is not evidence about the facing.
    extrude distance=3 direction=(0, 0, -1)

    # Kozo paper: warm off-white, never pure white. A 1.0 sheet clips flat under any
    # key light and the whole point of this model - a dihedral crease you can read as
    # a change of shade - disappears with it.
    set_vertex_color set_all=true color=(0.93, 0.90, 0.83, 1)

    uv channel=0 mode=xatlas
    split_normals split_angle=0
}

# Explicit elements, not `auto`: a crane is five slabs at five angles, and naming
# them is both cheaper and closer than any hull decomposition of the folded shell.
# Element at= / rotate= are in MESH space, after every part transform, and a box's
# local +X is tilted UP by a positive `pitch` - which is why the neck reads +42 and
# the tail reads -40 even though the tail rises. A box is symmetric about its centre,
# so -40 and +140 describe the same slab.
collision {
    complexity = simple_and_complex
    box size=(128, 12, 104) at=(0, 0, 50)                       # body keel
    box size=(150, 16, 30)  at=(104, 0, 106) rotate=(0, 42, 0)  # neck
    box size=(90, 16, 30)   at=(185, 0, 134) rotate=(0, -20, 0) # head and beak
    box size=(140, 16, 28)  at=(-102, 0, 100) rotate=(0, -40, 0)# tail
    box size=(94, 216, 46)  at=(0, 0, 87)                       # both wings
}
